In a cone of fixed shape the radius is a constant multiple of the height, so $V$ can be written in terms of $h$ alone.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2.6);
\coordinate (L) at (-2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2,0);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (L) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (R);
\draw (L) arc (180:360:2 and 0.55);
\draw[dashed] (L) arc (180:0:2 and 0.55);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (O) node[midway, left] {$h$};
\draw[dashed] (O) -- (R) node[midway, below] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A stone dropped into a pond makes a circular ripple whose radius increases at $4$ cm/s. At what rate is the area enclosed by the ripple increasing when the radius is $25$ cm? Leave your answer in terms of $\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\draw[dashed] (O) circle (2.6);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (O) -- (35:2) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

